Verdict: MAJOR  
Retrieval adequacy: insufficient

Findings:
- [major] The draft’s discoverability plan is incomplete for the providers it claims to cover. In `Contract-boundary rule`, `Artifact Map`, and `Files to Change`, the anchor set is `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.gemini/GEMINI.md`, and `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`, but it omits `.codex/CODEX.md`. Issue #2399 scope explicitly names `CODEX.md`, and `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` marks `.codex/` as a required provider adapter. This leaves Codex outside the stated “agents encounter the contract through existing control-plane entry points” requirement.
- [major] Retrieval is not adequate for the issue class. Issue #2399 is labeled `cat:harness`, and `docs/plans/README.md` says harness issues must consult `config/agents/` settings and `.claude/rules/`. The `Resource Intelligence Summary` cites standards, adapters, related issues, and one report, but no `config/agents/*` or `.claude/rules/*` surfaces. For a release-readiness contract, skipping the actual provider settings/config surfaces is a blocking evidence gap.
- [major] One cited source is not reproducible. `Documents consulted` lists `docs/reports/2026-04-20-refactor-knowledge-release-readiness-dependency-map.md`, but that file is not present on `main`. A plan cannot claim retrieval adequacy while relying on a non-retrievable source.
- [major] The plan does not actually define a reproducible evaluation corpus, even though the issue scope requires one. Issue #2399 calls for “evaluation corpus / golden tasks for comparing model releases,” but the `Artifact Map` and `Files to Change` only create `config/ai/model-release-smoke-battery.yaml` plus prose contracts/playbooks. There is no artifact for committed task fixtures, expected outputs, or baseline results, so future model-release comparisons will not be repeatable.
- [major] Hermes coverage is underspecified relative to the related work the plan itself cites. Issue #2399 scope includes “Hermes equivalents,” and related issue #1583 identifies concrete Hermes surfaces: `config/agents/hermes/*`, `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`, and machine rollout. None of those paths appear in `Artifact Map`, `Files to Change`, `TDD Test List`, or `Pseudocode`, so the promised ecosystem surface inventory is likely to miss the only explicit Hermes parity surfaces already tracked in-repo.

Missing tests:
- Discoverability coverage test that asserts all required provider entry points are anchored, including `.codex/CODEX.md` and Hermes-related surfaces from #1583.
- Fixture-based golden-task tests with expected outcomes for planning, adversarial review, repo navigation, code modification discipline, and session handoff integrity.
- Retrieval-completeness check for `cat:harness` plans to ensure `config/agents/*` and `.claude/rules/*` sources are cited before approval.

Scope creep concerns:
- `docs/standards/MODEL_RELEASE_SMOKE_RUNNER_CONTRACT.md` plus provider baseline inventory risks drifting into implementation/operations territory already adjacent to #2323 and #2089 unless the plan explicitly says this issue stops at contract + fixtures and does not build or operate the runner.

Weakest assumption:
- That a YAML battery spec and prose contracts are enough to satisfy the issue’s “evaluation corpus / golden tasks” deliverable.

Most likely implementation failure mode:
- The repo lands standards docs and a checklist, but the next provider release still has no executable, baseline-backed comparison corpus and no Codex/Hermes-complete entry-point coverage.

Most likely test gap:
- Session-handoff and cross-provider portability will be asserted in prose without committed fixtures or expected-output baselines, so drift will only be discovered after rollout.

Future issues suggested:
